{"source": "europepmc", "title": "Peak Expiratory Flow Rate as Predictors of Emergency Department Triage Level in Acute Exacerbation of Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease: An observational Cohort Study", "year": 2026, "doi": "10.21203/rs.3.rs-11531089/v1", "pmid": null, "pmcid": null, "openalex": "W7226029892", "journal": "Research Square", "authors": ["Torabi M", "Pormahyabadi R", "Bafghi NN", "Chegin M", "Mirzaee M"], "abstract": "Abstract Background Rapid, bedside identification of patients at highest acuity among those presenting with acute exacerbation of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (AECOPD) remains a core emergency department (ED) challenge. Objective To examine the association between peak expiratory flow measurements obtained at ED presentation and nurse-assigned Emergency Severity Index (ESI) acuity among adults presenting with AECOPD. Methods In this single-center observational cohort, 112 adults presenting with acute dyspnea attributed to AECOPD were classified by the treating triage nurse as ESI level 1 (n = 64) or level 2 (n = 48). Demographic characteristics, vital signs, venous blood gas measurements, and peak expiratory flow rate (PEFR) were recorded at presentation. PEFR was measured in triplicate and the highest reading was retained. Logistic regression was used to examine associations with nurse-assigned triage category. Discrimination was summarized with the receiver operator characteristic (ROC) area under the curve (AUC). Results Patients assigned to ESI level 1 had higher respiratory rates, lower oxygen saturation, lower measured PEFR and percent-predicted PEF ratio, and more frequent red-zone readings than patients assigned to level 2. They also had higher rates of bilevel noninvasive ventilation and intensive care unit admission and a longer length of stay. On multivariate analysis, respiratory rate (adjusted odds ratio [OR] 0.79 per breath/min, 95% CI 0.68–0.91), measured PEFR (adjusted OR 1.01 per percentage point, 95% CI 1.00–1.02), and PEF ratio percent-predicted (adjusted OR 1.07 per percentage point, 95% CI 1.03–1.12) independently predicted triage level. Models combining respiratory rate with measured PEFR or percent-predicted PEF ratio had reported AUCs of 0.85 and 0.86, respectively. Conclusion A simple bedside combination of respiratory rate and measured PEFR or PEF ratio percent-predicted discriminated ED triage level with good accuracy in patients with AECOPD. Incorporating peak flow meter readings into initial triage assessment may help standardize acuity stratification for this population.", "url": "https://europepmc.org/article/PPR/PPR1367692", "licence": "cc-by", "is_oa": true, "is_preprint": true, "tier": "A", "domains": ["A2_emergency_flow", "A3_inpatient_flow"], "retrieved_at": "2026-11-15T07:49:46+00:00"}
